Circagraph server for FreeBSD
=============================

Install
-------

As root:

    pkg install -y ./circagraph-server-freebsd<release>-amd64.pkg
    circagraph-setup

pkg installs what the package needs from FreeBSD's repositories: Node.js 24 (node24),
PostgreSQL @PG_MAJOR@, GMP and SQLite. Like FreeBSD's ports, the package starts nothing
itself (pkg ends whatever its scripts leave running); circagraph-setup finishes the
first installation:

  - creates the `circagraph` system user, and `circagraph-logs` for the syslog and
    GitHub receivers;
  - initializes PostgreSQL @PG_MAJOR@ if it is not yet (/var/db/postgres/data@PG_MAJOR@, with
    peer authentication on its local socket), and enables and starts it;
  - writes /usr/local/etc/circagraph/circagraph.env, with a new AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY;
  - creates the `circagraph` PostgreSQL role and database, with the bundled pgmp
    extension;
  - migrates the database (circagraph-migrate), and enables and starts the
    `circagraph` service, which listens on 127.0.0.1:5173.

circagraph-setup is safe to run again, for example if PostgreSQL could not be started
the first time.

Configure
---------

Edit /usr/local/etc/circagraph/circagraph.env, then `service circagraph restart`:

  - APP_ORIGIN: the public HTTPS origin users reach, without a trailing slash. The server
    refuses a plain http:// origin other than localhost; on a trusted private network
    without a proxy, set ALLOW_INSECURE_ORIGIN=1 and HOST=0.0.0.0 to serve
    http://<name>:5173 directly. The services log to syslog (/var/log/messages); if the
    website reports "Server misconfigured", the reason is there.
  - RESEND_API_KEY and AUTH_EMAIL_FROM for account email, then
    `sysrc circagraph_mail_enable=YES && service circagraph_mail start`.
  - ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD for the first administrator,
    created by `circagraph-migrate`. The password is used once; to sign in with it
    again (or with a new one written there), run `circagraph-reset-admin-password`.
  - Sign-in providers, human verification, quotas: see the comments in the file and
    authentication.md, administration.md in this directory.

Back up AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY apart from the database: without it, enrolled authenticator
secrets cannot be decrypted. Do not change it once accounts exist.

Serve it publicly through an HTTPS reverse proxy (www/caddy, www/nginx). With Caddy:

    timescale.example {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5173
    }

Caddy 2.5 and later replace an incoming X-Forwarded-For with the client address, so set
TRUST_PROXY=1 (with another proxy, only if it does the same; see README.md). Proxies
should allow request bodies of at least 32 MiB and streaming responses (live updates).

The syslog receiver (syslog.md) is configured in /usr/local/etc/circagraph/syslog.env
with routes in /usr/local/etc/circagraph/syslog-routes.json; then
`sysrc circagraph_syslog_enable=YES && service circagraph_syslog start`.
It runs as its own user, circagraph-logs, which cannot read circagraph.env or log
in to the database (#501): syslog.env is root:circagraph-logs, mode 0640, and so must
be the routes file (`chown root:circagraph-logs FILE && chmod 0640 FILE`). See
syslog.md, "Its own account".

`cg-import-logs` imports historic log files (log-import.md), with OCH_API_URL and
OCH_API_KEY from the environment or syslog.env: `cg-import-logs --help`.

The GitHub receiver (github.md) mirrors issues and pull requests into timelines from
GitHub's webhooks, on 127.0.0.1:5580 behind your HTTPS reverse proxy. It is configured in
/usr/local/etc/circagraph/github.env (the API key and the webhook's secret) with routes
in /usr/local/etc/circagraph/github-routes.json; then
`sysrc circagraph_github_enable=YES && service circagraph_github start`.
It runs as circagraph-logs too: github.env is root:circagraph-logs, mode 0640, and
so must be the routes file. It keeps its state in /var/db/circagraph-github.
`cg-import-github` imports a repository's existing issues and pull requests (github.md),
with OCH_API_URL, OCH_API_KEY and GITHUB_TOKEN from the environment or github.env:
`cg-import-github --help`.

Upgrade
-------

Installing a newer package (`pkg install -y ./circagraph-server-...pkg`) stops the server
and leaves the database and configuration as they are. It then lists:

  1. Settings this version's template has that the configuration lacks. Compare with:
         diff -u /usr/local/etc/circagraph/circagraph.env \
                 /usr/local/share/circagraph/circagraph.env.example
  2. A backup:  su -l postgres -c 'pg_dump circagraph' > circagraph.sql
  3. circagraph-migrate, which migrates the database and starts the server, and the mail
     worker (circagraph_mail), the syslog receiver (circagraph_syslog) and the
     GitHub receiver (circagraph_github), each if it is enabled. Disabled ones stay
     stopped.

circagraph-migrate is safe to run more than once. `--no-restart` migrates only and starts
nothing. Releases up to 0.12.1 started only the server; after upgrading from one, start an
enabled receiver with `service circagraph_syslog start` (#374).

Upgrading from a version before #501 moves syslog.env and the routes file it names
(SYSLOG_ROUTES) from the server's user to the log receivers' (circagraph-logs), keeping
their modes: only regular files with one link, reached through no symbolic link, in folders
only root can change. It lists what it moved and what it left for you to change.

From openchronology-server
--------------------------

Until 0.30 the package was openchronology-server. Install circagraph-server over it as
any upgrade, then follow what it lists:

    pkg install -y ./circagraph-server-freebsd<release>-amd64.pkg
    su -l postgres -c 'pg_dump openchronology' > openchronology.sql
    circagraph-migrate

Both packages have pgmp's files (and the old commands' names), so pkg removes
openchronology-server as it installs circagraph-server; with -y it goes ahead. Its
removal stops its services and prints what it left behind: ignore that note, for what it
lists is now Circagraph's. If pkg stops instead, remove it first (its configuration,
database and users stay) and install again:

    pkg delete -y openchronology-server

Then circagraph-server's installation (or circagraph-setup, if it could not) moves the
installation:

  - /usr/local/etc/openchronology becomes /usr/local/etc/circagraph, and
    openchronology.env in it circagraph.env; links stay at the old names.
  - The rc.conf settings openchronology_enable, openchronology_mail_enable,
    openchronology_syslog_enable and openchronology_github_enable (and their _config)
    become circagraph_enable, circagraph_mail_enable and so on, so the services that
    ran before start again with circagraph-migrate and at boot.
  - The installation keeps its system users, openchronology and openchronology-logs, its
    `openchronology` database and role, /var/db/openchronology and
    /var/db/openchronology-github: circagraph-setup sets circagraph_account=openchronology
    in rc.conf, and the services run as that user (and the receivers as its -logs user).
    circagraph-migrate and circagraph-reset-admin-password run as the configuration's
    group.

The commands are circagraph-setup, circagraph-migrate, circagraph-reset-admin-password,
cg-import-logs and cg-import-github; the old names (openchronology-migrate,
och-import-logs, ...) are links to them. The rc.d services have only the new names:
`service circagraph restart`, `service circagraph_syslog status` and so on.

Remove
------

`pkg delete circagraph-server` stops and removes the server. The configuration
(including AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY), the database and the system users remain; delete them with:

    sysrc -x circagraph_enable circagraph_mail_enable circagraph_syslog_enable \
      circagraph_github_enable
    rm -rf /usr/local/etc/circagraph /var/db/circagraph /var/db/circagraph-github
    su -l postgres -c 'dropdb circagraph && dropuser circagraph'
    pw userdel circagraph
    pw userdel circagraph-logs

For an installation from openchronology-server, the removal names its users and database
(openchronology), and also removes the link /usr/local/etc/openchronology and the
circagraph_account setting.

Layout
------

  /usr/local/lib/circagraph               the application and och-convert
  /usr/local/sbin/circagraph-migrate      database migration
  /usr/local/sbin/circagraph-setup        first-time setup, and the move from
                                          openchronology-server
  /usr/local/sbin/circagraph-reset-admin-password   the administrator's password, from
                                          the configuration
  /usr/local/etc/circagraph               configuration (created on first install)
  /usr/local/share/circagraph             configuration templates of the installed version
  /usr/local/etc/rc.d                     circagraph, circagraph_mail,
                                          circagraph_syslog, circagraph_github
  /usr/local/lib/postgresql, /usr/local/share/postgresql/extension   pgmp
